+++
title: Ugarit
description: Ancient port city in western Syria and northern levant
hook: Discovered in 1928, this coastal city in what is now northern Syria has
  yielded the oldest known systematic use of an alphabet — and texts in a
  language previously unknown to anyone, Ugaritic.
standfirst: Ugarit began in the Neolithic Fertile Crescent, occupied from the
  end of the 8th millennium BC and settled on through the Chalcolithic and
  Bronze Ages. In the Late Bronze Age (c. 1500 – c. 1200 BC) it grew into the
  Kingdom of Ugarit, and c. 1185 BC it was destroyed.
key-facts:
  - name: Built
    value: c. 7000 BC
    from: "built: c. 7000 BC"
  - name: Abandoned
    value: c. 1185 BC, in the Bronze Age Collapse
    from: "abandoned: c. 1185 BC event: Bronze Age Collapse"
  - name: Where it is
    value: Latakia Governorate, Syria, in the Fertile Crescent
    from: "location: Latakia Governorate, Syria region: Fertile Crescent"
  - name: Periods
    value: Neolithic, Late Bronze Age
    from: "periods: Neolithic, Late Bronze Age"
  - name: Excavated
    value: 1928–1939, 1950–2008
    from: "excavation-dates: 1928–1939, 1950–2008"
  - name: Visiting
    value: Ruins, publicly owned, open to the public
    from: "condition: Ruins owner: Public public-access: Yes"
pull-quotes:
  - text: "So you had written to me: “Could I not have demanded my needs [from]
      the Great King, the king of Egypt, my lord? I demand this request: [In]
      the land of Ugarit there is a severe hunger (bi-ru-ú dan-niš): May my
      lord save [the land of Ugarit], and may the king give grain (ZÍZ.AN.MEŠ)
      to save my life … and to save the citizens of the land of Ugarit.[[ ^24
      ]]"
    attribution: The ruler of Ugarit, quoted back in a letter from the Egyptian
      pharaoh Merenptah (r. 1213 – 1203 BC)
    section: destruction
  - text: "My father, behold, the enemy's ships came (here); my cities(?) were
      burned, and they did evil things in my country. Does not my father know
      that all my troops and chariots(?) are in the Land of [[ Hatti |
      hittites ]], and all my ships are in the Land of [[ Lukka ]]? \\...
      Thus, the country is abandoned to itself. May my father know it: the
      seven ships of the enemy that came here inflicted much damage upon us.[[
      ^27 ]]"
    attribution: Ammurapi, the last king of Ugarit, responding to the king of
      Alashiya's appeal for assistance
    section: destruction
  - text: "As for the matter concerning those enemies: (it was) the people from
      your country (and) your own ships (who) did this! And (it was) the
      people from your country (who) committed these transgression(s) \\... I
      am writing to inform you and protect you. Be aware![[ ^23 ]]"
    attribution: Eshuwara, the senior governor of Alashiya
    section: destruction
  - text: When your messenger arrived, the army was humiliated and the city was
      sacked. Our food in the threshing floors was burnt and the vineyards
      were also destroyed. Our city is sacked. May you know it! May you know
      it![[ ^23 ]]
    attribution: A letter written to the Hittite viceroy at Karkemiš after the destruction
    section: destruction
  - text: Thus Kušmešuša, king of Alašiya, say to Niqmaddu, king of Ugarit, my
      son. All is well with me, my households, my countries, my wives, my
      sons, my troops, my horses and my chariots.… In exchange of the gift
      which you had sent me, I sent to you thirty-three (ingots of) copper;
      their weight is thirty talents and six-thousand and five-hundred
      shekels.[[ ^85 ]]
    attribution: Kušmešuša, king of Alašiya, to Niqmaddu, king of Ugarit
    section: cuneiform-tablets
timeline:
  - when: c. 7000 BC
    what: The settlement is built.
    from: "built: c. 7000 BC"
  - when: the end of the 8th millennium BC
    what: The site is occupied, and stays a settlement through the Chalcolithic
      and Bronze Ages.
    from: The site was occupied from the end of the 8th millennium BC and
      continued as a settlement through the Near Eastern [[ Chalcolithic ]]
      and [[ Bronze Ages | bronze-age ]] until its destruction c. 1185 BC.
  - when: 6th millennium BC
    what: Wikidata's date for the city's inception.
    from: "inception: 6th millennium BC"
  - when: Around 1820 BC
    what: The Great Kingdom of Yamhad (Aleppo) emerges under King Sumu-Epuh;
      Ugarit would eventually become its vassal.
    from: Around 1820 BC (MB IIA), the [[ Great Kingdom of Yamhad | yamhad ]]
      (Aleppo) emerged under its King [[ Sumu-Epuh ]] as a regional power.
      Ugarit (Stratum 7) would eventually become a vassal of Yamhad along with
      several other petty kingdoms in the Northern Levant (Syria).[[ ^10 ]]
  - when: c. 1765 BC
    what: The Mari Archive mentions Ugarit.
    from: The !!Mari Archive!! (c. 1765 BC) mentions Ugarit.
  - when: around 1620
    what: Hattusili I of Hatti attacks Yamhad; raids by Mursili I around 1600/1590
      BC bring Yamhad down.
    from: In MB IIB (c. 1628 – c. 1590 BC), the Hattusili I of Hatti attacked
      Yamhad around 1620 with Mursili I of Hatti making new raids around
      1600/1590 BC, causing the Great Kingdom of Yamhad to collapse.
  - when: around 1400 BC
    what: Scribes in Ugarit appear to have developed the oldest systematically
      used alphabet, of 30 letters.
    from: Scribes in Ugarit appear to have developed the oldest systematically
      used [[  alphabet | ugaritic-alphabet ]] around 1400 BC; 30 letters,
      corresponding to sounds, were inscribed on clay tablets.
  - when: c. 1350 – c. 1315 BC
    what: Under Niqmaddu II, Ugarit becomes a vassal of the Hittite Empire.
    from: During the reign of his son [[ Niqmaddu II ]] (c. 1350 – c. 1315 BC)
      Ugarit became a vassal of the [[ Hittite Empire ]], first under a
      viceroy in [[ Karkemiš ]] and then, with the Hititte collapse, directly
      under Karkemiš.[[ ^17 ]][[ ^18 ]][[ ^19 ]]
  - when: 1194 BC
    what: Chancellor Bay's known term of office sets an upper limit on the year of
      Ugarit's destruction.
    from: "It was already established that Bay was in office from approximately
      1194 to 1192 BC. The two pieces of evidence together thus set an upper
      limit on the year of Ugarit's destruction: 1194 BC."
  - when: between 1192 and 1190 BC
    what: Radiocarbon dating, cuneiform records and a dated eclipse indicate the
      destruction.
    from: "[[ Radiocarbon dating ]] combined with translations of cuneiform
      records and the absolute dating of recorded astronomical events, e.g.,
      an [[ eclipse ]] in 1192 BC, indicate destruction between 1192 and 1190
      BC."
  - when: 1190s BC
    what: Wikidata's date for the city's dissolution.
    from: "dissolved-abolished-or-demolished: 1190s BC"
  - when: c. 1185 BC
    what: Ugarit is abandoned, in the Bronze Age Collapse.
    from: "abandoned: c. 1185 BC event: Bronze Age Collapse"
  - when: "1928"
    what: A peasant plowing a field accidentally opens an old tomb, and the
      forgotten site is found.
    from: After its destruction in the early 12th century BC, Ugarit's location
      was forgotten until 1928 when a peasant accidentally opened an old tomb
      while plowing a field.
  - when: "1929"
    what: The French Mission de Ras Shamra begins excavating, led by Claude
      Schaeffer.
    from: Beginning in 1929 excavations of Ugarit were conducted by a French team
      called the Mission de Ras Shamra led by archaeologist [[ Claude
      Schaeffer | claude-frédéric-armand-schaeffer ]] from the [[ Musée
      archéologique | musée-archéologique-strasbourg ]] in [[ Strasbourg ]].
  - when: "1939"
    what: Work is interrupted by the outbreak of World War II.
    from: Work continued until 1939 when it was interrupted by the outbreak of [[
      World War II ]].
  - when: "1950"
    what: The French excavation resumes, again under Claude Schaeffer, until 1970.
    from: The French excavation, now the Mission Archeologique Française de Ras
      Shamra-Ougarit, resumed in 1950, led again by Claude Schaeffer until
      1970.
  - when: "2011"
    what: Excavations are interrupted by the Syrian civil war.
    from: Excavations were interrupted by the [[ Syrian civil war ]], which began
      in 2011.
image-plan:
  - file: Ugarit_Corbel.jpg
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: The corbelled entrance to the [[ Royal Palace of Ugarit ]], a
      building constructed over several major phases between the 15th and 13th
      centuries BC.[[ ^74 ]][[ ^4 ]]
  - file: Ugarit_Tomba_-_GAR_-_3-01.JPG
    role: full
    section: late-bronze-age
    caption: A tomb in the Royal palace's courtyard. Beneath two of the palace's
      northern rooms lay three large corbel-vaulted family tombs, found devoid
      of any contents.[[ ^91 ]]
  - file: Baal_Ugarit_Louvre_AO17330.jpg
    role: inline
    section: ugaritic-literature
    caption: A [[ Baal ]] statuette from Ugarit. The [[ Ba'al tales | baal-cycle
      ]] detail Baal-[[ Hadad ]]'s conflicts with [[ Yam | yam-god ]] and [[
      Mot | mot-semitic-god ]] for pre-eminence under the God [[ El | el-deity
      ]].[[ ^31 ]]
  - file: Boar_rhyton_Louvre_AO18521.jpg
    role: inline
    section: archaeology
    caption: Mycenaean ceramic imported to Ugarit, 14th–13th century BC — the city
      traded with [[ Aegean | aegean-sea ]] states across its golden age.[[
      ^13 ]]
  - file: Text_of_Law_in_Akkadian_by_King_Niqmepa_with_dynastic_seal_Ras_Shamra_Louvre_Museum.jpg
    role: inline
    section: cuneiform-tablets
    caption: A text of law in Akkadian by King [[ Niqmepa ]], carrying the
      dynastic seal. From Ras Shamra; Louvre Museum.
  - file: Ugarit_Royal_Palace_archives_space_3912.jpg
    role: inline
    section: royal-palace
    caption: One of the palace's archive spaces. Tablets were found in archives
      located across the palace, from reports on outlying regions to practice
      writing by young scribes.[[ ^87 ]]
  - file: 5.9_Ugarit.jpg
    role: inline
    section: royal-palace
    caption: The palace reception hall. Rooms were arranged around courtyards,
      encompassing 6,500 square meters before the city's destruction.[[ ^74
      ]][[ ^4 ]]
  - file: 5.6_Ugarit.jpg
    role: inline
    section: acropolis
    caption: Ugarit. Only the highest—the newest—levels of the city's ruins have
      been excavated.
  - file: Ugarit_Dagan_aka_Dagon_temple_3971.jpg
    role: inline
    section: acropolis
    caption: The temple of [[ Dagon ]] on the acropolis, excavated between 1929
      and 1937.[[ ^73 ]][[ ^74 ]]
  - file: Dagan_temple_3D.png
    role: inline
    section: acropolis
    caption: A 3D reconstruction of the Temple of Dagan. Its foundation walls were
      thickest on the north side of the naos, at 4.4 metres; it is proposed
      this supported a stairway and a sacrificial site on the terrace.[[ ^92
      ]]
  - file: Maison_aux_alabaster.png
    role: inline
    section: the-house-of-alabaster-vessels
    caption: A 3D reconstruction of The House of Alabaster Vessels, in the
      residential quarter east of the royal palace, a location where high
      officials reside.[[ ^74 ]]
  - file: RasIbnHani,tomb1.jpg
    role: inline
    section: ras-ibn-hani-and-minet-el-beida
    caption: A tomb in the north palace at Ras Ibn Hani. A "royal palace", elite
      housing and tombs were found there, along with about 169 cuneiform
      tablets, most in the Ugaritic language.[[ ^97 ]][[ ^48 ]]
  - file: Sceau_Minet_el_Beida_AO15771.jpg
    role: inline
    section: ras-ibn-hani-and-minet-el-beida
    caption: "A steatite cylinder seal from Minet el-Beida: a warrior holding a
      head stuck on a pike, with a winged disc, a lioness, a twist, the bust
      of a man, globules, caprids around a tree and a hand. Cylinder seals
      were among the finds at the port.[[ ^104 ]][[ ^105 ]]"
  - file: TempleOfRhytonUgarit.png
    role: inline
    section: the-temple-of-rhyton
    caption: A 3D digital reconstruction of the Temple of Rhyton in [[ Blender |
      blender-software ]]. Its main hall is rectangular, roughly 6 × 7 metres,
      with benches and a stepped platform or [[ altar ]].[[ ^74 ]]
  - file: StatueOfEl.png
    role: inline
    section: the-temple-of-rhyton
    caption: A 3D digital reconstruction of the statue of [[ El | el-deity ]].
      Some scholars have argued the complex should be identified as the Temple
      of El on the basis of this limestone [[ cult statue | cult-image ]].[[
      ^106 ]]
glossary:
  - term: Rhyton
    gloss: A ceremonial drinking vessel for libation; a large number were found
      near the temple that is named after them.
    from: It is likely named after the large amount of [[ rhytons | rhyton ]]
      (ceremonial drinking vessels for [[ libation ]]) discovered in its
      vicinity.[[ ^5 ]]
  - term: Tell
    gloss: The mound a long-occupied site leaves behind; at Ugarit its southern
      slope is under orange groves, which prevents excavation.
    from: The southern slope of the mound, or [[ tell | tell-archaeology ]], is
      covered by orange groves, preventing excavation.[[ ^49 ]]
  - term: Pronaos and naos
    gloss: "The two parts of a temple: the porch (pronaos) and the sanctuary
      proper (naos)."
    from: Both temples are composed of a pronaos (porch) and a naos (sanctuary
      proper), aligned from north-northeast to south-southwest.
  - term: Ashlar
    gloss: Squared building stone; at Ugarit's palace, preserved ashlar blocks
      reach heights of up to 4 meters.
    from: The palace was well constructed, predominantly crafted from stone, with
      preserved ashlar blocks reaching heights of up to 4 meters.
  - term: Ugaritic King List (UKL)
    gloss: The king list that is essentially the only source for Ugarit's rulers
      before roughly 1350 BC; it originally carried 52 names.
    from: Because all texts found at Ugarit date between roughly 1350 BC and 1185
      BC, essentially all that is known about earlier rulers of Ugarit comes
      from the Ugaritic King List (UKL).
  - term: Dingir
    gloss: The cuneiform character that precedes each of the 52 names on the king
      list, indicating that the ruler had been deified.
    from: All 52 names are preceded by a [[ Dingir ]] character indicating that
      that ruler had been deified.
  - term: Glyptics
    gloss: Cylinder seals, scarabs, impressions and seal rings; at Ugarit they are
      decorative, religious, or royal.
    from: Glyptics, or cylinder seals, scarabs, impressions, and seal rings, were
      found to be of [[ Hyksos ]] and [[ Hittite | hittites ]] influence, and
      the types of glyptics being decorative, religious, or royal.[[ ^106 ]]
  - term: Marzēah
    gloss: A Levantine communal celebration in honour of a deity, accompanied by
      wine and music, and often descending into excess.
    from: In addition to the cultic function, the Temple has also been identified
      as a site for //Marzēah//, a [[ Levantine | levant ]] communal
      celebration in honour of a certain deity, including El. The bouquets are
      described as indulgent and abundant, accompanied by wine and music, and
      often descending into excess.
further:
  - slug: carchemish
    why: Karkemiš was the seat of the Hittite viceroy Ugarit answered to, and the
      last place Ammurapi begged for troops as the enemy advanced.
  - slug: mari-syria
    why: The Mari Archive of c. 1765 BC mentions Ugarit, and Zimri-Lim of Mari
      made a state visit; Mari correspondence is how we know Ugarit was active
      early in the millennium.
  - slug: byblos
    why: In the Amarna letter EA 89, Rib-Hadda of Byblos likens the palace at Tyre
      to the grandeur of the palace inside Ugarit's walls.
anchors:
  hook: "!!Ugarit!! ([[ /juːˈɡɑːrɪt, uː-/ |
    https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help%3AIPA%2FEnglish ]]; Ugaritic: [[
    𐎜𐎂𐎗𐎚 | https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/𐎜𐎂𐎗𐎚 ]], //ủgrt//
    /ʾUgarītu/) was an ancient [[ Levantine | levant ]] coastal city located
    in what is today northern [[ Syria ]], at a site known in [[ Arabic ]] as
    !!Ras Shamra!! or !!Tell Shamra!!. Since its discovery in 1928, the site
    has revealed the [[ oldest known systematic use of an alphabet |
    ugaritic-alphabet ]]. [[ Ugaritic texts ]] were written in a previously
    unknown [[ Northwest Semitic | northwest-semitic-languages ]] tongue—the
    [[ Ugaritic ]] language."
  standfirst: Ugarit has its origins in the [[ Neolithic ]] period of the [[
    Fertile Crescent ]]. The site was occupied from the end of the 8th
    millennium BC and continued as a settlement through the Near Eastern [[
    Chalcolithic ]] and [[ Bronze Ages | bronze-age ]] until its destruction
    c. 1185 BC.[[ ^3 ]][[ ^4 ]] During the [[ Late Bronze Age ]] (c. 1500 – c.
    1200 BC) the settlement saw significant growth, culminating in the
    establishment of the Kingdom of Ugarit.[[ ^5 ]]
