!!Ugarit!! ([[ /juːˈɡɑːrɪt, uː-/ | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help%3AIPA%2FEnglish ]]; Ugaritic: [[ 𐎜𐎂𐎗𐎚 | https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/𐎜𐎂𐎗𐎚 ]], //ủgrt// /ʾUgarītu/) was an ancient [[ Levantine | levant ]] coastal city in what is today northern [[ Syria ]], at a site known in [[ Arabic ]] as !!Ras Shamra!! or !!Tell Shamra!!. It lies 10 km (6.2 mi) north of the Syrian city [[ Latakia ]], and at its zenith it ruled an area roughly equivalent to the modern [[ Latakia Governorate ]].

Since the site was discovered in 1928 it has revealed the [[ oldest known systematic use of an alphabet | ugaritic-alphabet ]]. The [[ Ugaritic texts ]] found there were written in a previously unknown [[ Northwest Semitic | northwest-semitic-languages ]] tongue—the [[ Ugaritic ]] language.

Excavation shows people living on the site since the 8th millennium BC, but the digging has concentrated on the late [[ Bronze Age ]] levels, and relatively little is known about the earlier occupation.[[ ^1 ]][[ ^2 ]]

== History

Ugarit has its origins in the [[ Neolithic ]] period of the [[ Fertile Crescent ]]. The site was occupied from the end of the 8th millennium BC and continued as a settlement through the Near Eastern [[ Chalcolithic ]] and [[ Bronze Ages | bronze-age ]] until its destruction c. 1185 BC.[[ ^3 ]][[ ^4 ]] The growth came late: during the [[ Late Bronze Age ]] (c. 1500 – c. 1200 BC) the settlement expanded sharply, and out of that growth came the Kingdom of Ugarit.[[ ^5 ]]

The city had close ties to the [[ Hittite Empire ]], in later periods as a [[ vassal state ]]. It sent tribute to [[ Egypt | ancient-egypt ]] at times, and maintained trade and diplomatic relations with [[ Alashiya ]] (possibly [[ Cyprus ]]),[[ ^6 ]] as documented in archives recovered from the site and corroborated by [[ Mycenaean | mycenaean-greece ]] and Cypriot pottery found there. The polity was at its height from c. 1450 – c. 1185 BC,[[ ^3 ]] when it was destroyed, along with the sites of many other polities that fell in the [[ Late Bronze Age collapse ]] of 12th century BC. Both [[ Tell Sukas ]] and nearby [[ Tell Tweini ]] (proposed as ancient Gibala), a coastal city at the southern edge of the Kingdom of Ugarit, were destroyed at the same time.[[ ^7 ]]

== Neolithic, Chalcolithic Age

- Pre-Pottery Neolithic, Stratum 16
- Pre-Pottery Neolithic, Stratum 15
- Halafian Chalcolithic, Stratum 14
- Halafian Chalcolithic, Stratum 13
- Chalcolithic Ugarit Stratum 12

== Early Bronze Age

- EB I-II - Ugarit Stratum 11
- EB III - Ugarit Stratum 10
- EB IV - Ugarit Stratum 9

== Middle Bronze Age

A major drought event ended around 2020 BC, and the Middle Bronze Age emerged after it. In Mesopotamia, the Fall of Ur III led to the Isin-Larsa period.[[ ^8 ]]

== Middle Bronze I

The material from MB I comes from Ugarit's Stratum \8.[[ ^9 ]]

== Middle Bronze IIA

Around 1820 BC (MB IIA), the [[ Great Kingdom of Yamhad | yamhad ]] (Aleppo) emerged under its King [[ Sumu-Epuh ]] as a regional power. Ugarit (Stratum 7) would eventually become a vassal of Yamhad, along with several other petty kingdoms in the Northern Levant (Syria).[[ ^10 ]]

The !!Mari Archive!! (c. 1765 BC) mentions Ugarit. Mari was a kingdom on the Middle Euphrates, east of the Yamhad Empire, and the archive contained cuneiform tablets written in Akkadian. Trade would have gone from Ugarit, Aleppo, Emar, Mari down to Babylon. [[ Zimri-Lim ]] of Mari (r. 1775–1760 BC) also made a state visit to Ugarit.[[ ^11 ]]

Ugarit had contacts with the [[ Egyptian Middle Kingdom | middle-kingdom-of-egypt ]] (c. 2000 – c. 1700 BC). One artifact is a [[ carnelian ]] bead inscribed with the name of [[ Senusret I ]].[[ ^12 ]] A [[ stela ]] and a statuette from the Egyptian pharaohs [[ Senusret III ]] and [[ Amenemhet III ]] have also been recovered; it is unclear at what time these objects were brought to Ugarit.

== Middle Bronze IIB

In MB IIB (c. 1628 – c. 1590 BC), the Hattusili I of Hatti attacked Yamhad around 1620, with Mursili I of Hatti making new raids around 1600/1590 BC, causing the Great Kingdom of Yamhad to collapse. Ugarit (Stratum 6) was relatively shielded, lying on the coast behind a mountain range, while large parts of Inner Syria were devastated by the Hittite onslaught that ended MB IIB. In the Northern Levant there is no MB IIC (c. 1590 – c. 1550 BC) as in the Southern Levant; instead the material culture changes to LB IA.

== Late Bronze Age

The city reached its golden age between 1500 BC and 1200 BC, when it ruled a mercantile coastal kingdom trading with Egypt, Cyprus, [[ Aegean | aegean-sea ]] states (primarily [[ Minoan | minoan-civilization ]] [[ Crete ]]), polities in Syria, the Hittites, cities of the Levantine core (including [[ Ascalon ]] in [[ Canaan ]]), and much of the eastern Mediterranean.[[ ^13 ]] Five of the Egyptian [[ Amarna letters ]] from the mid-14th century BC were written in Ugarit.[[ ^14 ]] The population of Ugarit in this period is estimated to be between 7,000 and 8,000 individuals,[[ ^15 ]] and the kingdom controlled about 2,000 km^^2^^ on average.[[ ^16 ]]

In the mid-14th century BC, Ugarit was ruled by king [[ Ammittamru I ]]. A letter (EA45) sent by him, probably to pharaoh [[ Amenhotep III ]] (1388–1351 BC), expresses cordial diplomatic relations between the two. During the reign of his son [[ Niqmaddu II ]] (c. 1350 – c. 1315 BC) Ugarit became a vassal of the [[ Hittite Empire ]], first under a viceroy in [[ Karkemiš ]] and then, with the Hititte collapse, directly under Karkemiš.[[ ^17 ]][[ ^18 ]][[ ^19 ]] During the reign of [[ Niqmaddu III ]] Ugarit was one of several vassals which supplied troops to support the Hittites against the [[ Suteans ]] in a battle at [[ Tuttul ]] (possibly modern Tell Bi’a, though there were two cities with that name in the region).[[ ^20 ]] Diplomatic relations with Egypt continued, as two more letters show: one sent by Niqmaddu II (EA49) and one by his wife Ḫeba (EA48), probably sent to [[ Akhenaten ]] (1351–1334 BC). The former includes a request from the Egyptian king to send a physician to Ugarit.[[ ^14 ]][[ ^21 ]][[ ^22 ]]

== Destruction

From the late 13th century into the early 12th century BC the eastern Mediterranean faced severe and widespread food shortages, possibly from [[ crop failures | harvest#crop-failure ]] due to [[ climate change ]][[ ^23 ]] or [[ plant disease ]]. The kings of Ugarit—a major supplier and transporter of foodstuffs—received a number of desperate pleas for food from neighboring states. Eventually Ugarit itself faced shortage and hunger. A letter from Egyptian pharaoh [[ Merenptah | merneptah ]] (r. 1213 – 1203 BC) quotes back a message sent by the ruler of Ugarit:

<blockquote
  So you had written to me: “Could I not have demanded my needs [from] the Great
  King, the king of Egypt, my lord? I demand this request: [In] the land of
  Ugarit there is a severe hunger (bi-ru-ú dan-niš): May my lord save [the land
  of Ugarit], and may the king give grain (ZÍZ.AN.MEŠ) to save my life … and to
  save the citizens of the land of Ugarit.[[ ^24 ]]

The last king of Ugarit, [[ Ammurapi ]] (c. 1215 – c. 1180 BC), was a contemporary of the last known [[ Hittite | hittites ]] king, [[ Suppiluliuma II ]]. A letter by Ammurapi stresses the seriousness of [[ the crisis | bronze-age-collapse ]] faced by many [[ Near Eastern | near-east ]] states due to attacks from outsiders.[[ ^23 ]] Ugarit's army and navy joined with Hittite forces to stem the oncoming enemy; the allies eventually had to fall back from [[ Anatolia ]] to the Syrian frontier.[[ ^25 ]][[ ^26 ]] Ammurapi's response to the king of [[ Alashiya ]]'s appeal for assistance highlights the desperate situation in Ugarit and across the eastern Mediterranean:

<blockquote
  My father, behold, the enemy's ships came (here); my cities(?) were burned,
  and they did evil things in my country. Does not my father know that all my
  troops and chariots(?) are in the Land of [[ Hatti | hittites ]], and all my
  ships are in the Land of [[ Lukka ]]? \... Thus, the country is abandoned to
  itself. May my father know it: the seven ships of the enemy that came here
  inflicted much damage upon us.[[ ^27 ]]

Eshuwara, the senior governor of Alashiya, responded:

<blockquote
  As for the matter concerning those enemies: (it was) the people from your
  country (and) your own ships (who) did this! And (it was) the people from your
  country (who) committed these transgression(s) \... I am writing to inform you
  and protect you. Be aware![[ ^23 ]]

In the end Ammurapi begged for forces from the Hittite viceroy at [[ Karkemiš | carchemish ]]. The invaders had captured the kingdom's other port, [[ Ra’šu | ras-ibn-hani ]], and were advancing on the city of Ugarit.

<blockquote
  To the king, my lord say, thus Ammurapi, your servant.… I wrote you twice,
  thrice, [new]s regarding the enemy! … May my lord know that now the enemy
  forces are stationed at Ra’šu, and their avant-guard forces were sent to
  Ugarit. Now may my lord send me forces and chariots, and may my lord save me
  from the forces of this enemy![[ ^24 ]]

The viceroy sent troops to assist Ugarit, but Ugarit had already been sacked when the Hittite forces reached the city. A letter written to the viceroy after the destruction said:

<blockquote
  When your messenger arrived, the army was humiliated and the city was sacked.
  Our food in the threshing floors was burnt and the vineyards were also
  destroyed. Our city is sacked. May you know it! May you know it![[ ^23 ]]

== Language and literature



== Alphabet

Scribes in Ugarit appear to have developed the oldest systematically used [[  alphabet | ugaritic-alphabet ]] around 1400 BC; 30 letters, corresponding to sounds, were inscribed on clay tablets. The exact logic behind the design of Ugaritic letters is still unknown, and proving a single origin has remained difficult up until today. The letters show little or no formal similarity to the Phoenician, but the standard letter order (seen in the [[ Phoenician alphabet ]] as ʔ, B, G, D, H, W, Z, Ḥ, Ṭ, Y, K, L, M, N, S, ʕ, P, Ṣ, Q, R, Š, T) shows strong similarities between the two, suggesting that the Phoenician and Ugaritic systems were not wholly independent inventions.[[ ^28 ]] A [[ Unicode block | ugaritic-unicode-block ]] for Ugaritic has been defined.[[ ^29 ]]

== Ugaritic language

The existence of the [[ Ugaritic language ]] is attested to in texts from the 14th through the 12th century BC. Ugaritic is usually classified as a [[ Northwest Semitic language | northwest-semitic-languages ]] and therefore related to [[ Hebrew ]], [[ Aramaic ]], and [[ Phoenician | phoenician-language ]], among others, while its [[ grammatical | grammar ]] features are highly similar to those found in [[ Classical Arabic | arabic ]] and [[ Akkadian | akkadian-language ]]. It possesses two [[ genders ]] (masculine and feminine), three [[ cases | grammatical-case ]] for [[ nouns ]] and [[ adjectives ]] ([[ nominative ]], [[ accusative ]], and [[ genitive ]]); three numbers: ([[ singular | singulative-number ]], [[ dual | dual-grammatical-number ]], and [[ plural ]]); and [[ verb ]] [[ aspects | grammatical-aspect ]] similar to those found in other Northwest Semitic languages. The word order in Ugaritic is [[ verb–subject–object ]] (VSO) and [[ subject-object-verb ]] (SOV); [[ possessed–possessor | possession-linguistics ]] (NG) (first element dependent on the function and second always in genitive case); and [[ noun ]]–[[ adjective ]] (NA) (both in the same case (i.e. congruent)).[[ ^30 ]]

== Ugaritic literature

Apart from royal correspondence with neighboring Bronze Age monarchs, the Ugaritic literature from tablets found in the city's libraries includes mythological texts written in a poetic narrative, letters, legal documents such as land transfers, a few international treaties, and a number of administrative lists. Fragments of several poetic works have been identified: the "[[ Legend of Keret ]]", the "Legend of [[ Danel ]]", and the [[ Ba'al tales | baal-cycle ]] that detail [[ Baal ]]-[[ Hadad ]]'s conflicts with [[ Yam | yam-god ]] and [[ Mot | mot-semitic-god ]], for pre-eminence under the God [[ El | el-deity ]],[[ ^31 ]] among other fragments.[[ ^32 ]][[ ^33 ]]

== Rulers

All the texts found at Ugarit date between roughly 1350 BC and 1185 BC, so essentially all that is known about earlier rulers of Ugarit comes from the Ugaritic King List (UKL). It is, however, known from [[ Mari | mari-syria ]] correspondence that Ugarit was active earlier in the millennium, though it is not clear that the site maintained continuity of occupation in the intervening period.[[ ^34 ]][[ ^35 ]] The first UKL exemplar found, alphabetic [[ cuneiform ]] KTU 1.113 (RS 24.257), was written in the [[ Akkadian language ]], and only a fragment survives, with the names of only some of the rulers. It was determined that there were originally 52 names on the list. It was not clear if the list ran in direct or retrograde order.[[ ^36 ]][[ ^37 ]][[ ^38 ]]

Four more exemplars have since been found: syllabic cuneiform RS 88.2012, RS 94.2518, RS 94.2528 and RS 94.2501 (incomplete), also written in the Akkadian language. All 52 names are preceded by a [[ Dingir ]] character indicating that that ruler had been deified. Deification of rulers first appeared in the [[ Akkadian Empire ]] period and by the Old Babylonian period had become relatively common.[[ ^39 ]][[ ^40 ]] Attempts have been made to link one ruler entry, Krt, with the figure Kirta known from the literary composition "The Legend of Kirta".[[ ^41 ]]

A number of Late Bronze Age Ugarit rulers used a [[ cylinder seal ]] (not yet found) reading "Yaqarum, son of Niqmaddu, king of Ugarit". Yaqarum is the first, or in some interpretations the last, name on the UKL. It is assumed that this seal was an heirloom used to add legitimacy to the current ruler; an alternate view was that Yaqarum was a tradition mythical name for the Ugarit ruler.[[ ^34 ]][[ ^42 ]][[ ^43 ]] There were two exemplars of the seal, one well cut and dated to the Middle Bronze Age and one crudely cut and dated to the Late Bronze Age. Late Bronze rulers also occasionally used a ring seal.[[ ^44 ]]

